course: dictionary
lesson: 1512
title: Celtic: one word, many meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which meaning is meant. «Celtic» is one of these words. It can describe something, name a group of languages, or name a football club. Ask yourself: is it a thing, a language, or a team?
explain: «Halloween on 31 October grew from Samhain, an old Celtic festival at the end of the harvest.» Here Celtic describes the festival. The clues are old and harvest, so it is about the ancient Celts, long ago.
explain: «Celtic music» and «the Celtic languages» both use the word, but in different ways. Celtic music describes a style linked to Ireland, Scotland and Wales. In «the Celtic languages», the word points to a family of languages such as Irish and Welsh. Read the whole phrase to decide which meaning fits.
fact: Celtic can be an adjective, which describes things, like «Celtic music» or «a Celtic knot».
fact: Celtic can also name a family of languages: Irish, Scottish Gaelic, Welsh, Breton and Cornish.
fact: The Celts were ancient peoples of Europe. Today Celtic peoples and cultures live on in Ireland, Scotland, Wales, Cornwall, the Isle of Man and Brittany.
fact: Celtic is also the name of a famous football club in Glasgow, Scotland.
fact: Knots and spirals are patterns from ancient Celtic art, and you can still see them on jewellery and crosses today.
exercise: In «Halloween grew from Samhain, an old Celtic festival», what does Celtic describe? | *An ancient festival of the Celts | A type of music | A football team | A language
exercise: Which of these is a Celtic language? | Spanish | *Welsh | French | German
exercise: Celtic is the name of a famous football club in which city? | Manchester | London | *Glasgow | Cardiff
test: In «Celtic Britons», what does Celtic describe? | A football club | *The ancient peoples of Britain | A type of music | A language spoken in Italy
test: In «the Celtic languages», what does Celtic mean? | An ancient festival | A football club | A type of knot | *A family of languages, such as Irish and Welsh
exam: In «Celtic is playing at home on Saturday», what does Celtic mean? | A language | A pattern of knots and spirals | *The football club in Glasgow | An ancient festival
